= Polysemy — Technical =

== Sense architecture ==
Polysemy raises a representational question:

<pre>
one lexical entry + multiple stored senses?
one underspecified representation + contextual enrichment?
generative lexical rules?
structured conceptual network?
continuous contextual representations?
</pre>

Different lexical theories answer differently.

== Regular alternations ==
Systematic alternations challenge pure sense enumeration because similar meaning shifts recur across lexical classes.

Generative Lexicon and related approaches use typed lexical structures and coercion mechanisms; cognitive-semantic approaches often model senses as networks around prototypes or schemas.

== Psycholinguistic evidence ==
Reaction-time, priming, reading-time, and contextual expectation studies test whether related senses pattern differently from unrelated homonyms.

Results support meaningful distinctions while also showing that sense boundaries can be graded and task-sensitive.

== Contextualized models ==
Contextual language models produce token representations that vary with context. Clustering or probing these representations can reveal sense structure, but geometric separation in embedding space is not automatically a psychological sense inventory.

== Lexicographic caution ==
Dictionary senses are editorial segmentations optimized for users. They are evidence about usage but not a gold-standard ontology of the mental lexicon.

